venus 0119: haptics (AW8697) and cover/headset sensors

The iOS haptics engine had nothing to drive: the Mi 11 carries an Awinic
AW8697 haptic driver on I2C (0x5a, bus i2c@998000) and our tree had no
driver for it, and the HID event service had no output report path at all
(setElementValue returned kIOReturnUnsupported), so no userland request
could ever reach a motor.

Three pieces:

  - bsd/dev/sweet_haptic.c: the AW8697 driver.  The QUPv3 I2C blocks need
    GENI firmware that only the bootloader may have left behind, so the
    driver bit-bangs I2C on the two bus pins (TLMM 28/29) instead of using
    the QUP sequencer; the same trick the touch driver uses for SPI.  The
    tuning values are the ones the venus device tree carries
    (vib_cont_drv_lev, vib_cont_td, vib_cont_zc_thr, vib_cont_num_brk,
    vib_tset, vib_r_spare, vib_bstdbg, vib_bemf_config, vib_f0_pre,
    vib_f0_coeff).  Every register write is read back and verified, and
    the motor is only ever started through the GO register, which is
    cleared again after the requested time.  Off by default
    (sweethaptic=0); sweethaptic=1 turns it on.

  - iokit/Kernel/arm/hid/SweetHaptics.cpp: the haptic service (sensor
    page 0x20, usage 0x68 "Haptic Vibrator") and the output report path:
    setElementValue() now reaches handleOutputElement(), which the haptic
    service turns into a buzz.

  - iokit/Kernel/arm/hid/SweetCoverSensor.cpp: the hall switch (TLMM 80,
    active low, the "smart cover" sensor of the stock Xiaomi device tree)
    and the USB-C audio accessory state, published as a sensor page
    service with usage 0x69 "Hall Effect Switch".

The property names of the cover/headset service are a guess: no Apple
phone has a hall switch, so there is no reference to copy them from.  See
logs/research/impl/17-haptics-cover.md.

